WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:06.570
If you’ve just watched The Matrix or you’re
a Californian who has gone a little overboard

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:11.620
on the weed legalization celebrations, you
might be wondering; how do I know that I’m

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.740
not just part of a computer simulation?

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:20.130
Does Super Mario think it’s pretty standard
to be attacked by suicidal turtles all day?

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:25.400
Maybe Kimmy from Candy Crush, never wonders
why she isn’t riddled with diabetes.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:31.220
It might seem obvious to us that they are
completely virtual but who’s to say that

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.140
we aren’t part of a giant simulation?

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.350
Maybe we’re all NPCs in a computer game
So, let’s take the red pill and the blue

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:50.230
pill at the same time, because screw you Morpheus,
as we ask ourselves; is anything real?

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:56.019
Thanks to science fiction, we often associate
simulated realities with computers, but doubting

00:00:56.019 --> 00:00:58.819
reality is far from a new thing.

00:00:58.819 --> 00:01:04.910
The Greek philosopher Plato, who lived around
2500 years ago, wrote about the concept in

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:06.930
his work the Republic.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.460
He pictured a cave with prisoners chained
to the wall and all they ever saw were the

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.210
shadows of others passing in front of a fire,
projected up onto the wall in front of them.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:23.729
This is the only life the prisoners had ever
known and so they would understandably believe

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:26.229
that the shadows were actual people.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:31.180
It’s similar to the modern world where we
look mainly at people’s virtual lives, though

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.579
social media, and we think of their actual
lives as far more exciting than our own, when

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:40.509
in fact they are having the same terrible
time that we all are having.

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:44.090
The real problem comes with this definition
of “I”.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:50.759
“cogito ergo sum” – I think therefore
I am – but that doesn’t help us prove

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:52.520
WHAT you are.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.894
Master Chief exists but he’s just a bunch
of code I can control…

00:01:58.894 --> 00:02:04.579
John Locke, pointed out that we could never
actually know the real essence of anything,

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:10.300
we could only deal with its nominal essence,
meaning what we actually experience of it,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.870
rather than the whole picture.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:16.709
He said it was like looking at a clock face;
all you can see is the time it shows, you

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:21.570
don’t have any idea what cogs and wires
are behind it, making it work.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:27.450
So as much as we believe we know how the universe
works behind the scenes, we’re still really

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.800
just clueless puppets.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.350
Everything you experience comes in through
your various senses, such as your sense of

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:36.560
smell, sense of touch, etc.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:42.610
But at the end of the day, all of these senses
end up as electrical impulses in your brain.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:49.420
Daniel Kish had his eyes removed at just 13
months old, due to retinal cancer, and from

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:55.450
a young age he trained himself to echolocate,
using clicks with his tongue and listening

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:56.630
for the echo.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:01.150
He became so proficient at this that he was
able to mountain-bike and tell the difference

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.660
between surfaces such as metal and wood.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:10.319
And when neurologists tested him, they found
that his visual cortex was being activated,

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:18.400
so he was really seeing objects using sound,
not just hearing them, the brain had just

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.870
swapped around some of the inputs.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:26.239
Your body also senses an astonishing amount
of data, more than you could ever realistically

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:27.239
process.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.690
So if you wanted to create a fake reality,
you wouldn’t need to program in every tiny

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:38.790
detail, since only a small fraction would
be experienced by the user at any one time.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:43.200
Right now there is air pressure acting on
every millimeter of your body, there’s light

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.900
bouncing off objects from countless things
outside of this screen you’re staring at,

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.420
and that strange pain in your elbow… well
I don’t know what that is and you should

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.030
probably get it checked out.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:58.730
And this is why we have pain, it’s like
a red flag that makes you pay attention to

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:04.689
one specific area of your body, because if
you listened to all parts of your body, all

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:09.730
the time, your brain would simply be overwhelmed
with information.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.750
So then, let’s take this a step further.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:17.191
If perception is just electric impulses in
your brain then your brain is also just a

00:04:17.191 --> 00:04:21.760
lump of quite fancy meat, full of electrical
impulses.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.260
Any idiot with some bacon and a Taser can
recreate that.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.539
So surely your consciousness is more than
just the material that makes up your brain,

00:04:30.539 --> 00:04:32.259
isn’t it?

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:35.539
We call these things; substrate independent.

00:04:35.539 --> 00:04:39.910
This means something that is more than the
stuff it is made up of.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.640
Waves are a good example of this.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:49.150
If you see a wave on the ocean, it has amplitude,
frequency, things like that.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:55.440
And these properties are not a part of the
sea water, they are part of the wave and independent

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.440
from the water.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:02.540
So the bacon and Taser combination may produce
some similar matter to a brain but it’s

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:07.250
not going to create the same conscious thoughts
like “what is love?” and “what was that

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.280
thing I was supposed to be doing right now?”
and “why don’t electric eels electrocute

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:13.280
themselves?”

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.280
…Seriously, why is that?

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:19.160
So even if your brain isn’t meat, if it’s
just a sting of code written by some scientist,

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:25.880
or alien, or a 2038 iphone app, that’s not
to say that your consciousness isn’t a real

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.190
thing.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:34.380
All your ideas could be unique and original…
okay, relatively original at least.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:41.900
Coding doesn’t mean that your every thought
has already been decided, just that the initial

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.669
conditions have been set.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:47.380
And this leads us to our own AI.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:53.080
As we build more and more advanced programs,
it makes the likelihood that we’re in a

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.169
simulation seem even higher.

00:05:56.169 --> 00:06:02.729
Just think about it, if we can code simulated
brains, what’s to say we aren’t already

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:04.090
part of one.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:10.849
Elon Musk once said it’s only about a one
in a billion chance that we’re not in a

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:11.849
simulation.

00:06:11.849 --> 00:06:13.919
And he’s a pretty switched-on guy.

00:06:13.919 --> 00:06:19.889
In fact, it may only be by creating our own
simulations that we are able to really test

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:21.389
our own reality.

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:27.770
Physicist Silas Beane, is one of many theorists
who has said that a simulation must have limited

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:32.539
computing power; it can only get as big as
the hardware will allow it.

00:06:32.539 --> 00:06:38.190
So, if we start creating enormous simulations
of our own and those create other enormous

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:43.300
simulations, at some point we’re going to
use up all the processing power available

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:50.030
to whatever hardware our universe is running
on and we’re going to crash it.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:55.360
Until that happens, or we somehow discover
the inner working of our reality, we can never

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.580
truly know for sure if everything we know
is just a simulation.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:18.180
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

